      <!-- Detailed Review: Top Producer -->
      <div id="top-producer" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:16px;padding:32px;margin-bottom:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:white;font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:16px;">Top Producer</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Top Producer is one of the oldest names in real estate software, running for well over two decades and now owned by FiveStreet (Constellation). Its core product is a real estate CRM built around MLS data. The Pro plan is $179 per user per month and includes the CRM and Market Snapshot reports, automated SMS and email lead response, built-in transaction management, and direct integration with 320+ MLS boards. Branded Market Snapshot reports and MLS depth are genuinely its strongest cards, and they are hard to match. For an agent whose whole workflow centers on MLS data, or a brokerage already standardized on Top Producer, it does a real job.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The pricing adds up fast, though, and this is where we have to be straight with you. The $179 Pro plan does not include a website, lead routing, or IDX. A website is an extra $35 per user per month and FiveStreet lead routing is about $25 per user per month, so a working stack starts around $239 per user per month before any lead package. Pro + Leads starts from $479 a month (varying with lead volume) and Pro + Farming from $599 a month (varying with farm size). Team plans run $399 to $1,199 a month. It is all per user, all every month, all on annual contracts.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The reviews tell a consistent story, and it is not flattering on three points. First, the lead packages. The same complaints repeat across Trustpilot, Software Advice, and G2: guaranteed leads that do not arrive, leads outside the target area, numbers that do not connect, and no conversion. One Trustpilot reviewer wrote that they paid three months of $300 and got about 16 leads, most of them bad numbers or people who said the call was a scam. Second, the contracts. Reviewers say there is no cancel option on the website or in the software, that cancellation staff take days to call back, and that they were billed for months after they tried to leave. Third, the product itself: Capterra and SelectHub reviewers call the interface dated, clunky, and slow to adapt, with email features that are frequently criticized. Capterra shows 4.0 out of 5 from 323 reviews, but G2 shows just 3.1 out of 5 from 124 reviews. If the MLS data and market reports are your priority, Top Producer earns its place. If you are buying it for the leads, read the reviews first.
